g the include  files 'h_def.h' and 'h_edidef.h'.$SCREEN-INTERFACE  Termcap, Zterm, etc. (<40 chars, mandatory if not raw-I/O)  refer to paragraph 'HARDWARE'; I apologize for not using termcap.$OS-VERSION  Required/supported OS versions (<80 chars, mandatory if dependent)  developed using V2.0$RUNTIME-FILES  Paths of the files required at run-time (no limit, optional)  uses /r0 for temporary file; may be changed in 'h_def.h'$DOCUMENTATION-FILES  Paths of documentation files (no limit, optional)  hist.doc$SOURCE-LANGUAGE  Programming Language(s) used (<30 chars, mandatory)  C$SOURCE-AVAILABILITY  Avaliability of source code (<30 chars, mandatory)  public$SOURCE-FILES  Paths of the source files (no limit, optional)  h_edit.c  h_expand.c  h_main.c  h_rw.c  h_term.c  h_def.h  h_edidef.h  h_var.h$COMPILATION  How to compile the program (no limit, optional)  make                   for Wyse-50 emulation (Steinmann&Frei), mc68000  make -f=make.mcterm    for original terminal driver mc68000  edit sources (esp. *.h), then use makefile$AUTHOR-NAME  Name(s) ONLY of the author(s). (<=80 chars, mandatory)  Helmut Hoheisel-Zimmermann, D-2300 Kiel 14$AVAILABILITY  Public, Shareware, Commercial ? (<=30 chars, mandatory)  public$CONDITIONS  Conditions, e.g. Shareware fee (no limit, optional)  no military use$COPYRIGHT  Copyright information (<=80 chars, optional)  by H. Hoheisel$DISTRIBUTOR  Where can the program be obtained from ? (<=80 chars, mandatory)  EFFO$HISTORY  The program's version/revision history (no limit, recommended)  first developed on CP/M-68k in 1987, then ported to OS-9/68k$EXPANSION  Planned development for next version  no expansion planned by the author$INSTALLATION  How to install the program  (no limit, optional)  may be taken as the initial process while logging in ( -> password )$RESTRICTIONS  What definitely will NOT work (mandatory if any)  chd (chx) using wildcards doesn't work$EXPERIENCES  Experiences with the program (no limit, optional)  The author has developed this program using the CP/M-68k OS, ported it  to OS-9/68k using the genuine terminal driver of the mc68000.  That version was hardware dependant because it used a special memory  location to allow insert mode while editing the commandline.  All worked fine (as far as I can remember). After getting the Wyse-50  emulation of Steinmann&Frei the program was adapted to this terminal.   Hardware dependecy could be removed because insert mode can be switched  on and off by Escape sequences. Shortly after this adaption I got the  'ZSH' written by Lukas Zeller, which I use since that time (about 1.5  years ago) instead of my own history/editing aid, because it seems  more powerful to me.